  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 211 AM CDT Sat Sep 5 2026\n\nIt's an unseasonably warm start to our Saturday with \ntemperatures across most of the area still sitting in the middle\nto upper 70s as of 2 AM. A cold front is gradually inching \nsouth across the area as we speak. The placement of the front \nisn't terribly defined, but it looks to be draped roughly along \nthe I-88 corridor. There isn't much of a cold advective response\nimmediately behind the boundary, but slightly cooler temps in \nthe lower 70s are found north of the front across our northern \nCWA and up into southern WI. A few thunderstorms traversed our \nnorthern CWA along the boundary late last evening driven by a \nmidlevel shortwave impulse scooting east atop the area. That \nwave has since moved away to the east and taken the precip \ncoverage with it, but a reservoir of elevated instability \nremains over the area and it's possible that a light shower or \neven isolated thunderstorm develops along the boundary early \nthis morning as it progresses south. A general lack of forcing, \nhowever, will largely inhibit precip development this morning. \nWe are seeing some areas of fog developing in the wake of the \nfront up in southern WI. Nothing too dense has been observed nor\nis expected, but it's possible that some visibility reductions \nspread into northern IL early this morning north of I-80.\n\nMixing into relatively cooler, northerly BL flow today will \nkeep temperatures a couple to a few degrees cooler than \nyesterday with highs forecast in the middle 80s to lower 90s \nacross inland areas. The warmest temperatures and highest \ndewpoints are forecast in our far southern CWA where afternoon \nheat indices may approach 105. Expectations are for largely \ncloser to 100 though, so opted against issuing any heat \nheadlines for today. Elsewhere across inland areas, forecasting \nheat indices throughout the 90s today. It'll be a different \nstory near the lakeshore where the onshore flow will bring \nnoticeably cooler conditions than yesterday with afternoon \ntemperatures forecast in the 70s up to a few miles inland from \nthe lake.\n\nThe breezy northerly fetch will also generate choppy conditions\non the lake this evening into early tomorrow. Will continue to \nadvertise a Moderate Swim Risk for now as waves generally look \nto top out in the 2 to 4 foot range, but expectations are not \nfar from warranting a High Swim Risk for a period this weekend,\nespecially at northwest Indiana beaches.\n\nThe midlevel ridge will center itself over the region early \nnext week shunting any meaningful baroclinicity well away for a \nfew days. Highs in the lower 80s are forecast for tomorrow and \nMonday with mostly sunny skies. Upper 80s and lower 90s are \nfavored again for the middle part of next week with scattered \nchances for rain returning as early as Tuesday night.\n\nDoom",
